def add(id,title,deps,statement,strategy,kind='lemma'):
 if id in rows:raise ValueError(id)
 x={'id':id,'kind':kind,'title':title,'deps':sorted(set(['def-axiom-of-choice','def-dependent-choice']+deps)),'statement':'Assume AC and DC. '+statement,'strategy':strategy,'design_row':'AG-BIR-1','local_addition':True,'provenance':{'statement':'literature-derived','proof':'ai-altered'},'sources':{'references':refs},'dependency_level':0};idx=next(j for j,y in enumerate(a[0]['items']) if y['id']=='thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities');a[0]['items'].insert(idx,x);rows[id]=x;return id
stable=add('lem-double-plus-simple-cubic-rational-surface-branch-terminates','The double-plus-simple cubic surface branch terminates',['lem-square-tangent-conic-blowup-singularities-controlled-by-a-cubic','lem-nonsquare-tangent-conic-rational-surface-blowups-terminate','lem-equicharacteristic-fixed-coordinate-blowup-chain-defines-formal-arc','lem-normal-complete-surface-nonsingular-formal-arc-blowups-terminate','lem-surface-completion-base-change-preserves-closed-fibre-local-completions','thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings','thm-blowup-base-change-flat'],r'''Let $A$ be a rational Gorenstein normal local surface in the permitted canonical-module setting, with normal completion and generators $\mathfrak m=(x,y,z)$. If $z^2+a xy^2\in z\mathfrak m^2+(x,y)^4$ for a unit $a$, all its singular point-blowup branches terminate. A continuing square branch has unchanged residue field and the same $x$ defining every successive exceptional divisor.''',r'''Write an exact relation z²+a xy²+b x²z+c xyz+d y²z+e x⁴+f x³y+g x²y²+h xy³+i y⁴=0, after absorbing z²m into a unit coefficient and dividing by it. The reduced controlling cubic is āXY²: its simple X=0 root has a nonsquare successor; its only possible continuing square successor is the rational point u=y/x=v=z/x=0. Divide by x² in that chart to obtain v²+a xu²+b xv+c xuv+d xu²v+e x²+f x²u+g x²u²+h x²u³+i x²u⁴=0. Its quadratic is V²+b̄XV+ēX². If nonsquare, the existing case-I lemma terminates. If square choose a residue lift δ with b̄=2δ̄ and ē=δ̄², and put w=v+δx. Then b′=b−2δ and e′=e−bδ+δ² belong to the old m, hence b′=xB,e′=xE on this chart. The full new equation is w²+a xu²+c xuw+d xu²w+x²[Bw+(f−cδ)u+(g−dδ)u²+h u³+i u⁴]+x³E=0. Its cubic modulo w is X(āU²+ρXU+σX²), with ā≠0. X=0 is simple, so any multiple closed root is unique, rational, and lies at (X:U)=(1:ε). It satisfies āε²+ρε+σ=0 and 2āε+ρ=0, characterizing a double polynomial root in every characteristic without dividing by 2. Replace u by u−εx, using a local residue lift. The same x remains unchanged; the cubic becomes āXu², while all other cubic terms are divisible by w and all degree-at-least-four terms belong to w(m′)²+(x,u)⁴. Thus the broad invariant is preserved, with unit coefficient. Every continuing square step uses the x-chart, has the same residue field and has centre ideal generated by the same x; both shifts preserve that ideal. An infinite such branch would give the nonsingular formal arc and surjective completion map supplied by the direct-union helper. Normal completion makes its height-one kernel regular, so the formal-arc generator/exponent termination lemma contradicts an infinite singular branch; closed-fibre completion comparison transfers this regularity to the original sequence. Simple-root side branches terminate by case I. The exact coefficient expansions, root conditions and chart normalization are retained in square-conic-closure.md, §1; no quartic term or coordinate correction is suppressed.''')
triple=add('lem-triple-cubic-rational-surface-branch-reduces-in-two-steps','A triple-cubic surface branch reduces after two successors',['lem-square-tangent-conic-blowup-singularities-controlled-by-a-cubic','lem-rational-gorenstein-surface-tangent-conic-and-hilbert-function',stable,'lem-nonsquare-tangent-conic-rational-surface-blowups-terminate','lem-rational-normal-surface-point-blowup-normal-and-fibre-cohomology'],r'''For a rational Gorenstein normal local surface in the permitted setting with square tangent conic, if its nonzero controlling cubic is a scalar times a cube, its continuing branch enters the nonsquare case or the double-plus-simple cubic class after at most two successive square successors. This allows every characteristic and residue field.''',r'''Choose generators so the cubic is āY³ with ā≠0. Group the relation as z²+a y³+β x²z+γ x⁴∈J1=(x³y,x²y²,xyz,y²z), a unit, absorbing z²m into a unit coefficient. The sole possible singular x-chart successor has quadratic P(X,V)=V²+β̄XV+γ̄X²; if nonsquare it is case I. If square, choose δ lifting β̄=2δ̄,γ̄=δ̄² and make the old-ring correction w=z+δx². This preserves J1 because xyz changes by x³y and y²z by x²y². The new x²w and x⁴ coefficients belong to m. Expanding them in (x,y,w), absorbing a w² coefficient into a unit, and grouping terms gives z²+a y³+ρx³y+σx⁵∈J2=(x³z,x²y²,xyz,y²z). Write its right side Ax³z+Bx²y²+Cxyz+Dy²z. Its exact x-chart relation is v²+a xu³+ρx²u+σx³−Ax²v−Bx²u²−Cxuv−Dxu²v=0. The origin is singular: otherwise x is in the maximal-ideal square (the quotient by x has the double-line cotangent generators u,v), and this equation puts the square of a regular parameter v in the third power, impossible. Its quadratic is v², and its controlling cubic is X²(ρ̄U+σ̄X). Normality of its next rational blowup forces this cubic nonzero by the generic exceptional-DVR argument, so ρ or σ is a unit. If ρ is a unit its cubic is double-plus-simple, and an invertible linear choice of simple coordinate ρu+σx and double coordinate x puts it in the preceding lemma. If ρ is a nonunit, σ is a unit and ρ=xr on the chart. Swap X=u,Y=x,Z=v. The full relation becomes Z²+(σ+rX)Y³+aX³Y−AY²Z−BX²Y²−CXYZ−DX²YZ=0. Its last four terms lie in (X³Z,X²Y²,XYZ,Y²Z); hence it is already the corrected relation with new ρ=a a unit and new σ=0. One more blowup reaches the double-plus-simple class. In particular z²+x³+y⁵ transforms to v²+y u³+y³, then after the swap has new ρ=1; its next chart is w²+X s³+X²s, whose cubic X²s enters the stable class. The proof uses neither division by 2 or 3 nor a geometric-factorization assumption. The complete old-ring square correction and T1/T2 equations are in square-conic-closure.md, §2.''')
rdp=add('thm-rational-gorenstein-normal-surface-singularity-resolved-by-point-blowups','Rational Gorenstein normal surface singularities resolve by point blowups',['lem-rational-gorenstein-surface-tangent-conic-and-hilbert-function','lem-square-tangent-conic-blowup-singularities-controlled-by-a-cubic',stable,triple,'lem-nonsquare-tangent-conic-rational-surface-blowups-terminate','lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','lem-rational-singular-point-blowup-canonical-pullback-surjective','lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality','lem-regular-base-dualizing-traces-compose-on-rational-modifications'],r'''A rational Gorenstein normal local surface domain in the permitted canonical-module setting with normal completion is resolved by finitely many ordinary blowups at singular closed points. Every model is normal and rational, with invertible canonical module; the terminal model is regular and projective over the local base. It is unchanged off the original closed point.''',r'''Rationality makes each ordinary point blowup normal and propagates to its local rings. At a rational singular point, trivialize the local invertible canonical module; canonical pullback is a surjection from O onto a torsion-free generic-rank-one module, hence an isomorphism, so all singular successors remain Gorenstein. Their completions are normal by the permitted finite-type normal-completion helper. Nonregular points have the plane-conic tangent ring from the Hilbert-function lemma. If its quadratic is nonsquare, case I terminates. If square, its nonzero controlling cubic has singular successors only at its finitely many closed roots. Simple closed roots give nonsquare successors. A multiple closed root of a cubic has degree one and is unique; its factorization is either double-plus-simple or a cube, handled by the two preceding full chart lemmas. The triple case enters a stable or nonsquare branch after at most two continuing square successors, and the stable branch terminates by the same-exceptional-parameter arc argument. Thus no infinite singular-successor branch exists. The tree is finitely branching (at most three closed cubic zeros at a square point and at most one successor in case I). If it were infinite, repeatedly choosing a child with infinitely many descendants would give an infinite path, using the declared AC/DC. Hence it is finite. Blow up those finitely many singular points in their ancestor order. Locality and the preceding preservation statements give a regular terminal surface, normality at every step, projectivity and the isomorphism off the original closed point. No ADE classification diagrams or unproved square-persistence assertion are imported.''',kind='theorem')
convert=add('lem-complete-normal-surface-regular-resolution-converts-to-normalized-point-blowups','A complete normal surface resolution converts to normalized point blowups',['cor-complete-local-domain-finite-over-a-regular-power-series-ring','lem-normalized-point-blowups-dominate-local-normal-surface-modifications','lem-regular-local-surface-is-rational-by-point-blowup-domination','lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','lem-rational-normal-surface-reduced-to-invertible-canonical-module',rdp,'lem-local-normalized-point-blowup-sequences-spread-at-closed-points','lem-surface-open-regular-locus','lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','lem-proper-source-to-separated-target-proper'],r'''If a complete equicharacteristic normal local surface domain $A$ admits a proper birational regular model, then it admits a regular terminal model obtained by finitely many normalized point blowups. The centres can all be chosen singular; the normalizations are finite.''',r'''Choose the finite regular power-series subring R of A. Let Z→Spec A be a regular proper birational model, and choose a normalized-point sequence W over A dominating Z. W is projective over A and R, with finite normalizations, by the exact domination helper. Its finite singular set follows from the open regular locus and normality in codimension one. W→Z is proper since W is proper over A and Z separated; a closed singular w therefore maps to a closed z. The modification dimension helper gives both local rings dimension two. O_Z,z is regular and rational, and rationality propagation makes O_W,w rational in their common fraction field. W has the regular-base projective canonical module; the local canonical principalization helper reduces these finitely many rational points to invertible canonical modules by ordinary singular-point blowups. Their normal completions follow from the exact permitted formal-fibre helper. The rational-Gorenstein resolution theorem then resolves them by further ordinary singular-point blowups. Spread all finite local sequences at those closed points to W. The terminal scheme is regular over each original singular point and on the unchanged regular open, so globally regular. Composing its ordinary blowups with the initial normalized-point sequence gives the required normalized-point sequence over A. If an initial centre was regular, its entire descendant region remains regular by regular point-blowup preservation. Delete that regular-centre step and all later centres over it; retained centres lie in its unchanged open complement and their point blowups glue with the identity on the deleted regular region. Repeat for the finitely many such subtrees. The final regular model still exists, with only singular centres. No general alteration equivalence, mixed-characteristic theorem or formal-gluing theorem is used.''')
rows[convert]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[convert]['deps']+['lem-blowup-of-closed-point-of-regular-surface-is-regular','lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality']))
complete=add('thm-complete-equicharacteristic-normal-surface-resolution-by-normalized-point-blowups','Complete equicharacteristic normal surfaces resolve by normalized point blowups',['cor-complete-local-domain-finite-over-a-regular-power-series-ring','lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-finite-integral-closure','lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','lem-finite-separable-normal-surface-extension-preserves-bounded-h1','lem-complete-regular-surface-degree-p-extension-has-bounded-h1','lem-regular-local-surface-is-rational-by-point-blowup-domination','lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','lem-rational-normal-surface-reduced-to-invertible-canonical-module',rdp,convert,'lem-finite-normal-surface-cover-completed-local-degree-bound','lem-normalized-surface-point-blowup-resolution-descends-from-completion','lem-local-normalized-point-blowup-sequences-spread-at-closed-points','lem-surface-open-regular-locus','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective'],r'''Every complete equicharacteristic Noetherian normal local domain $A$ of dimension two admits a regular resolution by finitely many normalized point blowups at singular closed points. All normalizations are finite and the resulting morphism is projective, birational, and an isomorphism off the original closed point.''',r'''Choose a finite injective regular complete power-series subring R and argue strongly by the finite fraction-field degree D, for every such finite pair R⊂A. Degree one gives A=R because R is normal. If a proper intermediate field L exists, its normalization B over R is finite and normal in L; since it is a domain finite over complete local R, the finite-completion-factor decomposition has just one factor, making B local and complete. Its degree is smaller than D, so induction gives a regular projective normalized-point model Y over B. Normalize the dominant component of Y×_B Spec A. It is finite over Y (the fibre product is finite and its normalization is finite in the permitted class), projective over R and A, normal, and birational over A with finite cover degree [Frac A:L]. Its finite singular set consists of closed points. At each singular x over y, y is closed because the cover is finite; the local ring of the regular modification Y has dimension two. The completed-local degree-bound helper makes A_x^hat finite over a regular completed local power-series ring with degree at most [Frac A:L]<D. Apply the strong induction hypothesis there. Completion descent gives a local normalized-point resolution, and closed-point spreading integrates the finitely many sequences to obtain a regular proper birational model over A. The conversion helper gives a regular normalized-point sequence over A. If no proper intermediate field exists, the extension is separable or purely inseparable of degree p: choose a nonseparable element; its separable p-power would give an intermediate field unless in the base, and a purely inseparable degree larger than p would also have one. In the separable case the separable transfer helper gives bounded H1 from regular rational R; in the degree-p case the differential-trace helper does so. Maximal-H1 reduction gives a normalized-point model with rational closed local rings. Canonical principalization reduces its finite singular set to invertible canonical modules, and the now proved rational-Gorenstein resolution theorem resolves the remaining points. Spreading those finite ordinary blowup sequences gives a regular normalized-point model over A. Every used model has finite normalization, open regular locus and normal local completion by the already closed permitted-base endpoints; no general excellence theorem is substituted for them. Finally delete regular-centre subtrees as in the conversion lemma, preserving a regular terminal model and leaving only singular centres. The induction is well founded because every completed local cover in the intermediate case has strictly smaller degree. All models stay projective by finite-normalization projectivity.''',kind='theorem')
t=rows['thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities'];t['deps']=sorted(set(t['deps']+[complete]));t['statement']=t['statement'].split('\n\n**Step-1 status:')[0]
t['strategy']=r'''For every singular closed point y of the normal finite-type field surface Y, its local ring has dimension two and normal equicharacteristic completion by the exact regular-locus and formal-fibre helpers. The complete-normal-surface resolution theorem gives a finite normalized point-blowup resolution of that completion; its proof uses the finite regular power-series degree induction, the separable and degree-p trace bounds, rational canonical principalization, and the corrected Lipman triple-cubic/stable-cubic termination proved in the new branch lemmas. Normalization commutes with completion because the completion map has geometrically regular fibres: the finite normal chart algebra stays normal, embeds in Frac of the completed base and is finite birational over the base-changed unnormalized chart. Thus matching closed-fibre points and flat blowup base change descend the finite normalized sequence to the original local ring. Its terminal regularity descends by proper completion transfer. The normal surface has open regular locus and is regular in codimension one, so its singular set is a finite set of closed points. The local-to-global helper spreads the finite local sequences successively at those points, leaving each other region unchanged; the final finite global normalized-point sequence is regular everywhere, proper and birational over Y, and an isomorphism on its original regular locus. Its normalizations are finite by the permitted finite-type normalization helper, and Y0=Y because the original surface is normal. No properness over the field is used. Over a perfect field regular finite-type schemes are smooth by the actual published supplier; over an imperfect field the claimed conclusion is regularity. All characteristic cases are covered: square/root corrections use polynomial square and repeated-root identities without dividing by 2 or 3; triple-cubic normality forces a unit alternative and reduces the E8 successor to the stable fixed-coordinate arc case. The durable exact chart proof is square-conic-closure.md. No arbitrary-Noetherian alteration equivalence, unproved ADE classification or cap obstruction enters.'''
# The boundary remark now states the proved commissioned surface scope.
rem=rows['rem-surface-contraction-and-resolution-scope-boundaries'];rem['statement']=rem['statement'].replace('Resolution of normal integral finite-type surfaces over arbitrary fields remains a Step-1 escalation pending complete local proof assembly; the arbitrary-Noetherian equivalence of Stacks 54.14.5 is an uncommissioned strengthening removed by owner scope ruling.','Resolution of normal integral finite-type surfaces over arbitrary fields is proved by the exact local argument in [[thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities]]; the arbitrary-Noetherian equivalence of Stacks 54.14.5 is an uncommissioned strengthening removed by owner scope ruling.').replace('The commissioned surface theorem has no characteristic restriction, but is not yet closed here.','The commissioned surface theorem has no characteristic restriction.');rem['deps']=sorted(set(rem['deps']+['thm-resolution-of-normal-surface-singularities']))
